      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 0,
      "outputTokens": 0,
      "costUsd": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Last kind. A different tank's inflow is read every two minutes: three, five, six, four and two litres per minute, from t equals zero to eight. Estimate the water added. There's no formula, so we can't integrate it exactly. Use the trapezoidal rule. Each trapezium joins two neighbouring readings with a straight line. The strips are two minutes wide, so h is two. The ends count once, and the inside readings count twice. That's two over two, times three, plus two times fifteen, plus two, which is thirty-five. So about thirty-five litres flowed in. It's an estimate, because we don't know the rate between readings."
